WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:03.238
ROY FULLER: We're good to go.

00:00:03.238 --> 00:00:04.280
NEVIN HEINTZE: All right.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.110
Thank you very much, Roy.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:07.700
And thanks, everyone,
for your time,

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.610
and welcome to this session
on Spanner migration.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:15.323
So good afternoon to everyone
in the West Coast, and evening

00:00:15.323 --> 00:00:16.490
if you're on the East Coast.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:20.720
And I'm actually from
Sydney, so it's morning here.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:21.650
I'm Nevin Heintze.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.860
I'm a senior engineer at
Google working on Spanner.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:28.700
And my focus is on
database migration.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.960
So today, we're going to
discuss migration to Spanner.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:38.890
And I'm going to give an
overview of typical steps

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:39.917
in a migration.

00:00:39.917 --> 00:00:41.500
And then we're going
to do a deep dive

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.080
into some of the core
parts of a migration.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.290
And then I'm going to describe
best practices and also

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:48.580
some migration tools.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:49.620
Now just a heads-up.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.130
I think we advertised
this as 30 minutes.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.940
I probably have actually
about 25 minutes of material.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.882
So with questions,
just sort of a heads-up

00:00:57.882 --> 00:00:59.590
and an apology in
advance, we're probably

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:02.170
going to spill a little
bit over the 30 minutes.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.840
So we might get to 35 or
40 minutes, so apologies

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.340
in advance.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:12.350
OK, so here's a typical set
of steps in a migration.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:16.430
And these steps are fairly
independent of the source

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.080
and target databases involved.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.060
And I'm sure for some
of you, these steps

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.220
will be very familiar.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.380
What I'm going to do
is go over these steps

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.580
and highlight them from the
perspective of migrations

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:29.240
to Spanner.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.540
And I'm particularly going to
focus on migrations from MySQL

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.550
to Spanner.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:38.480
Most migrations start with
some kind of assessment phase.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.300
And the goal here is to
determine if the target

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.130
database-- in this
case, Spanner--

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.150
is likely to be a good fit.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.450
Will it provide the
necessary functionality?

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.690
Will it meet the
cost-performance requirements?

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:53.240
How much work will
the migration be?

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:54.840
What are the biggest risks?

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.750
What are the biggest benefits?

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:01.700
The next step is often
a proof of concept.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.700
And in many ways, this
is sort of an extension

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:05.880
of the assessment.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.970
So it's typical for
a proof of concept

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:11.210
to focus on key items that
come from the assessment,

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:13.190
like what are the biggest risks?

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:16.250
Or confirming that the main
benefits of the migration

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:17.310
can be delivered.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.120
So for example,
many Spanner users

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.050
see Spanner's ability to scale
as one of its main benefits.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:28.250
And so their POCs often focus
on evaluating Spanner's ability

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.700
to scale for their use case.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.920
The next step, step
3 on this slide,

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.870
is the core part of the
migration preparation,

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:41.230
so the design and
implementation of the migration.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:43.050
This can have many components.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.250
But there are three
common themes.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:48.420
The first is designing an
appropriate Spanner schema.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.180
The next is determining
how data will

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.290
be moved from MySQL to Spanner
and building components

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:56.820
to support this move.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.460
And the third is
application changes

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.280
to switch over to using Spanner.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:05.910
This can range from migrating
queries, to code changes,

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:10.260
to switching from MySQL's
APIs to using Spanner's APIs.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:14.220
And it could potentially involve
application modernization.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:19.170
The next step, step 4,
is testing in dry runs.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.340
Now successful migrations
involve significant testing,

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:26.970
either using test traffic or
parts of your production load.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.450
And it's common for these
two steps, steps 3 and 4,

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.430
to be intermixed.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:35.880
So you might have an initial
design and an implementation,

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:38.400
try it out, find
issues, and then go back

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.300
and do some redesign,
change some implementation.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.050
Maybe you find a
performance issue and you

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.820
need to go back and
tweak the schema,

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.070
and, for example, add an index.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:52.680
So this is really an iterative
process between steps 3 and 4.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:57.690
Many migrations have a
target day for moving data

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:00.660
or for completing the
move and switching over

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:01.800
to using Spanner.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.290
So this becomes a
really critical day,

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.270
a convergence of
all of the planning

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.910
and execution phases
of a migration.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.650
And there's usually
a substantial amount

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.080
of planning around what
happens on this day,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:17.399
with detailed schedules, kind
of all hands on deck situation.

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:19.230
And a key part of
this is knowing

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:23.130
how to respond if things
don't go according to plan.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.010
What signals and dashboards
should we be looking at?

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:27.930
What's the criteria
for reverting?

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.360
How do we revert?

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.860
I want to emphasize that all
of the steps I've listed here,

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:36.720
you shouldn't take this
as too prescriptive,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.660
because every migration
is going to be different.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.310
And which steps are
important, which

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:42.810
have the biggest
risks, which take

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:45.000
the most time will
vary significantly

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.640
from one migration to another.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.460
So over the next few
slides, I'm going

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.055
to do a deep dive into
each of these steps.

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:01.680
So as we discussed
earlier, most migrations

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.440
start with some kind
of assessment phase.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.140
And there's a basic
question we want to answer.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.840
Is Spanner a good choice?

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.360
A common starting
point is an analysis

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.300
of what MySQL
features are actually

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:19.000
being used, what types, and
indexes, and queries are used.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.340
What about stored
procedures, stored functions?

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.340
What about auto-increment?

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.960
And based on this
analysis, we need

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.470
to determine how well these
features map onto Spanner.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:31.980
So Spanner supports
most core types,

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.440
but not some of the
more exotic types--

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.540
geometry types and point types--

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:38.080
that MySQL has.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:40.710
Spanner also doesn't
support stored procedures.

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.980
And the usual solution there
is to pull that functionality

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.170
into application code.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:48.150
Auto-increment and
sequences are not

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:49.480
supported in Spanner, either.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:51.420
And these are
challenging to handle

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.300
at scale because of the hot
spots they can generate.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.660
We recommend using
random generation of IDs

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.340
to spread the load
across the key space,

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:02.640
and there are several
other alternatives.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.150
There are differences
between the query dialect

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:07.410
of MySQL and Spanner.

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:10.290
So for some migrations,
query translation

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:13.930
is a really critical component.

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:17.470
It's worth emphasizing that
there's a huge variation here

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:20.520
between different MySQL users.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:24.840
Some use a very broad
set of MySQL features,

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:28.980
and others essentially only
use MySQL as a key-value store,

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:32.430
just using very basic types.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:35.970
One trend we've observed
from MySQL users who

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:38.610
want to migrate to Spanner
is that they typically

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:42.450
have very large MySQL
databases and they're hitting

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:44.520
MySQL scalability limits.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:49.590
They're often using sharding and
have multiple MySQL replicas.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:53.370
As a result, they often limit
their use of MySQL features

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:56.010
that don't scale well,
such as stored procedures,

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:57.630
triggers, auto-increment.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:00.900
And they often have very highly
optimized queries, especially

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.820
for their core high
QPS operations.

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:07.110
So this often minimizes the
role of complex queries,

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:10.960
complex joins, and
full table scans.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:14.470
I want to briefly touch
on transaction semantics

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:16.000
and locking.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.120
So those of you who
are familiar with MySQL

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:22.570
will know that this is a
really complicated topic.

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:26.380
The default MySQL isolation
level is repeatable reads,

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.680
and most MySQL servers
run with this default.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.750
This isolation
level does, indeed,

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:33.520
provide repeatable
read semantics

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:35.870
for standard SQL queries.

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:38.110
However, the picture gets
much more complicated

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:42.100
for DML statements and for SQL
queries that require locks.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:45.340
Such statements are
essentially executed

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.230
using read committed semantics.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:50.290
So it's not uncommon
for MySQL users

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:53.350
to employ sophisticated
locking strategies

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:56.020
to ensure the transaction
semantics they need

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:59.170
or to implement safety checks
to identify and correct

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:01.930
anomalies due to, for
example, lost updates

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:04.750
or read or write skew.

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:09.430
In contrast, Spanner provides
a simple, robust transaction

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:11.440
model, which is serializability.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:14.020
So all transactions
appear as they

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:16.270
were executed in
serial order, even

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:19.510
if some of the reads and
writes of distinct transactions

00:08:19.510 --> 00:08:21.230
actually occur in parallel.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:24.310
In other words, anomalies
like lost updates,

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:27.490
random reads, read skew,
write skew, and so forth don't

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:28.930
happen in Spanner.

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:32.110
This can significantly
simplify code for managing logs

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:36.549
or for managing MySQL
transaction anomalies.

00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:40.150
Note that transactions
do occasionally fail,

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:43.240
and are rolled back in Spanner,
and have to be retried.

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:46.780
So it's really
important that your code

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:49.870
base and the libraries you use,
that together, they provide

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:51.640
the appropriate retry logic.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.010
OK, so another important
aspect of assessment

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:02.450
is looking at how your
applications communicate

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:03.980
with MySQL.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:06.000
What drivers do they use?

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.520
Do they use ORMs?

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:09.980
Are those supported by Spanner?

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:13.430
Finally, an assessment
usually contains an analysis

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:15.020
of the benefits
of using Spanner.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:16.640
So what are the
key features that

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:18.380
are driving the
adoption of Spanner?

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:20.810
It might be scalability,
or that Spanner

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:23.270
is a fully managed
service with no downtime,

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:26.780
or it may be just the high
availability of Spanner

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:30.170
that provides four 9's up
time for regional instances

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:34.080
and five 9's up time for our
multi-region configurations.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:37.190
We'll talk a little
more about HarbourBridge

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:40.760
later, but I want to
note here that one

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:42.950
of the core features
of HarbourBridge

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:45.050
is that it automatically
generates an assessment

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:50.510
report from a MySQL database

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:53.900
So moving on to step
2, as we noted before,

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:57.680
it is typical for
a proof of concept

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:00.120
to almost be an extension
of an assessment.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:05.210
So the role of the POC is often
to scope out the biggest risks,

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:06.980
validate the main benefits.

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:08.990
And it's quite common
for a Spanner posse

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:11.060
to focus on scalability,
to establish

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:14.840
that Spanner can scale to
your needs and workload.

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:17.210
Given Spanner's
virtually unbounded

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:20.330
scalability capabilities,
demonstrating this

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:23.420
is not usually an
issue, although we

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:26.300
have seen some cases
where hidden limitations

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:29.000
or constraints on
the customer's side

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.490
can lead to apparent
scalability limits.

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:35.390
And then it's a matter
of debugging a system end

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:38.180
to end to find out where
in the customer's stack

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:44.190
the issue is, or in network
configuration, and so on.

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:48.140
Another aspect of the POC is
figuring out sizing estimates.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:51.200
So how many Spanner nodes will
be required for the workload?

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:54.770
Sizing of any database
is workload-dependent,

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:57.210
and Spanner is no exception.

00:10:57.210 --> 00:10:59.840
We provide some rules of
thumb for basic workloads.

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:03.590
But we always strongly
recommend that you run tests

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:04.760
with your workload.

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:07.010
And this is often
part of the POC.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:14.530
Implicit in most POCs is
learning about Spanner.

00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:18.040
So it's common for our users to
get valuable Spanner experience

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:21.580
during the POC, and build their
level of comfort and confidence

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:24.280
that Spanner will work for them.

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:27.610
A couple of high level planning
aspects of a POC that are worth

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:28.370
mentioning.

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:30.100
So it's really
important to establish

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:32.650
a clear scope of the POC.

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:36.130
Don't try to address all
issues and solve all problems.

00:11:36.130 --> 00:11:38.050
But it's critical
to limit the scope

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:39.910
to just the important issues.

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:42.250
And of course, to have a
well-defined and realistic

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:43.750
success criteria for the POC.

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:51.660
The next step, step
3, is the core part

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:54.720
of the migration preparation,
the design and implementation

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:56.730
of the migration.

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:58.770
There's a lot we
could talk about here,

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:01.260
but I'm going to focus
on three common tasks--

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:03.780
schema migration,
data migration,

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:05.330
and application migration.

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:09.480
And we're going to talk
about schema migration first.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:12.990
That is, what we want to do
is design an efficient Spanner

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:16.560
schema to meet the application
and workload needs.

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:19.260
The starting point here is
usually types, and columns,

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:22.950
and tables to support
the application needs.

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:25.170
But really, critical
elements of the design

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:26.740
are around primary keys.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:28.920
So these have to be
designed for scalability,

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.440
and in particular
to avoid hot spots.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:33.330
And of course, they need
to be designed to be

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:35.040
appropriate for the query load.

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:37.680
So it's not uncommon
for problems

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:40.290
to surface months
after a migration that

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:43.690
can be tracked back to issues
with primary key design.

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:47.700
So this is a really
critical thing to get right.

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:51.160
Much of schema design is
driven by query performance.

00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:54.450
So the role of secondary
indexes is very critical.

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:57.300
And Spanner provides some
unique capabilities to optimize

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:00.690
queries-- so for example, using
interleaved tables to control

00:13:00.690 --> 00:13:03.690
the layout of data
between related tables--

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:06.960
and this can provide significant
query performance boosts

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.165
for joins, as well
as for writes.

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:16.190
Inherent in schema design
is managing trade-offs,

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:20.360
such as the cost of reeds
versus the cost of writes.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:23.690
For example, I might
add a secondary index

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:26.360
to improve my read
performance, but this

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:30.180
means that my writes will
become more expensive.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:33.380
So in Spanner, a
secondary index is

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:35.880
implemented as a Spanner table.

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:39.140
So when I add a secondary
index to a table,

00:13:39.140 --> 00:13:43.130
then any row I add to that
table actually adds two rows.

00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:45.530
It adds one row
to the base table

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:48.480
and another one to
the index table.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:50.990
So if I have many
secondary indexes,

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:53.360
then my writes can
become very expensive.

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:56.270
And it's really critical to
balance these trade-offs.

00:13:56.270 --> 00:13:59.030
And this is particularly
true if you're

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:02.000
moving from MySQL, where
the balance in costs

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:06.240
between indexes and reads
is a little different.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:09.200
So this is something that,
coming from a MySQL background,

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:10.700
pay particular attention to.

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:17.220
The next task we're going to
discuss is data migration.

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:20.380
And we could have a whole
workshop on data migration,

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:23.220
so I'm only going to skim over
some really high level choices

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:24.970
here.

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:27.940
In short, there are
many alternatives.

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:30.520
But in most cases,
business needs

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:34.570
or application architecture will
quickly narrow down the choices

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:35.380
that you have.

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:38.000
So I'm just going to
start with the simplest,

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:41.650
most basic migration strategy,
which is stop and copy.

00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:43.780
So obviously, a
big downside here

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:45.760
is that you need to
stop your application

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:48.640
for a period of time,
maybe a few hours or more.

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:51.400
And the basic idea is
stop everything, copy over

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:53.320
all the data from
MySQL to Spanner,

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:55.270
and then startup your
applications again.

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:58.190
So the beauty of this
approach is that simplicity.

00:14:58.190 --> 00:15:01.480
However, most online
databases can't

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:03.880
be migrated this
way, because they

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:05.290
can't tolerate the downtime.

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:09.670
But if you can, seriously
consider doing stop and copy.

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:12.580
The more common
approach, particularly

00:15:12.580 --> 00:15:16.270
for migrating online databases,
is the so-called zero downtime

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:16.970
approach.

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:20.890
So in this approach, you
first take a snapshot

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:24.250
of your MySQL database and
you start copying it over

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:25.110
to Spanner.

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.780
While that's ongoing, you
capture all of the real-time

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:31.210
changes using Change
Data Capture--

00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:32.470
CDC.

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:34.540
And you can either
replay them to Spanner

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:36.580
while the initial
load is ongoing,

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:40.330
or you can store the
CDC stream and replay it

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:43.520
after the initial
load is complete.

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:47.110
There are some
trade-offs involved here

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:49.660
and there are also some
important data consistency

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:50.770
issues to get right.

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:53.590
For example, if the
initial load and CDC

00:15:53.590 --> 00:15:56.170
writes happen at
the same time, you

00:15:56.170 --> 00:15:59.290
need to ensure that old
data from the initial load

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:03.280
doesn't overwrite incoming
new data from CDC.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:07.330
In any case, when the
initial snapshot load and CDC

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:09.940
load are complete,
there is a short period

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:12.490
when you stop the
MySQL database,

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:15.400
let the CDC stream
drain to Spanner,

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:17.980
and then switch over
applications to Spanner.

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:20.080
This switchover time
can be a few minutes

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:21.190
to maybe tens of minutes.

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:24.265
So there's some downtime, but
it's typically fairly small.

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:30.990
Another approach I want to
briefly mention is dual writes.

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:33.690
So in this case, you
modify the application

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:38.230
stack to write to both MySQL
and to Spanner at the same time.

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:41.430
So this also requires an
initial load of data to Spanner

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:45.210
and some synchronization between
the initial load and the dual

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:47.850
writes to ensure data integrity.

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:49.860
The main benefit
of this approach

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:54.960
is that it allows you to run
both databases in parallel

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:57.030
and to switch from
MySQL to Spanner,

00:16:57.030 --> 00:16:59.050
and back again, if needed.

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:03.361
However, this can be pretty
costly in terms of resources.

00:17:03.361 --> 00:17:05.069
And there are some
other really difficult

00:17:05.069 --> 00:17:08.410
technical issues to resolve,
and that's around failures.

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:12.510
So what happens if you're
committing a transaction

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:15.180
and the transaction
succeeds in one database

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:17.099
and fails in the other?

00:17:17.099 --> 00:17:19.740
How do you recover?

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:21.899
I see the question.

00:17:21.899 --> 00:17:25.079
Let me postpone
that until the end

00:17:25.079 --> 00:17:28.650
and I'll get back
to that question.

00:17:28.650 --> 00:17:32.130
Another approach is
incremental migration.

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:35.610
And this is not a
fully general solution,

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:38.670
but it can be really useful
for some applications.

00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:41.260
And I've seen this used in a
number of different contexts.

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:43.560
So the core starting
point is to have

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:45.400
a highly partitioned database.

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:49.830
So for example, the database
might map IDs of some kind

00:17:49.830 --> 00:17:54.150
to data about those IDs,
and where each ID is

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:55.830
independent of other IDs.

00:17:55.830 --> 00:18:00.780
So in particular, we need
for a update to the database

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:03.030
to involve just a single ID.

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:05.730
In other words, we don't
have any multi-ID update

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:07.190
transactions.

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:10.890
Then what you can do
is you can pick an ID,

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:13.950
copy its data from
MySQL over to Spanner,

00:18:13.950 --> 00:18:16.200
and that's usually
pretty quick, maybe even

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:18.010
a few tens of milliseconds.

00:18:18.010 --> 00:18:23.190
And then from that point on,
you serve that ID from Spanner.

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:27.690
And if needed, you can actually
move this ID back from Spanner

00:18:27.690 --> 00:18:29.520
to MySQL.

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:33.600
The core of this approach
is an ID-to-database mapping

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:35.640
that has to be maintained
so that you know,

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:38.660
at all times, which
database actually

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:39.660
owns that particular ID.

00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:48.820
The next step I want to
mention in the design

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:52.540
and implementation phase
is application migration.

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:55.060
And again, this is a
really large topic,

00:18:55.060 --> 00:18:57.230
and I'm just going to
touch on a few aspects.

00:18:57.230 --> 00:19:00.740
So the first is around
application code changes.

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:04.510
So what will be needed here
is going to heavily depend

00:19:04.510 --> 00:19:07.540
on what drivers the
application uses

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:09.320
and whether an ORM is used.

00:19:09.320 --> 00:19:14.470
So some database users
structure their applications

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:17.210
around a database
abstraction layer,

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:21.250
which is an
application-specific set of APIs

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:23.290
that abstract the
core operations

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:25.360
needed from the database.

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:29.140
And this can be a huge
help for migration,

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:32.470
since it localizes the
database-specific code

00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:34.810
to a small set of APIs.

00:19:34.810 --> 00:19:37.360
So application
migration can now focus

00:19:37.360 --> 00:19:40.450
on just rewriting those
APIs or the implementation

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:42.910
of those APIs to use Spanner.

00:19:42.910 --> 00:19:45.790
But the other extreme,
some applications

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:49.250
spread their database code
throughout the application.

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:52.630
And this can lead to really very
time-consuming and complex code

00:19:52.630 --> 00:19:54.700
migrations.

00:19:54.700 --> 00:19:59.620
We briefly mentioned before
that MySQL and Spanner have

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:01.900
different SQL query dialects.

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:04.690
So each query used
in an application

00:20:04.690 --> 00:20:06.250
needs to be checked
and potentially

00:20:06.250 --> 00:20:08.200
rewritten to use with Spanner.

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:10.378
So sometimes, this is
fairly straightforward.

00:20:10.378 --> 00:20:12.170
Sometimes, it can be
a huge amount of work.

00:20:12.170 --> 00:20:15.080
It depends on the structure
of the application.

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:18.410
Finally, I wanted to briefly
mention stored procedures.

00:20:18.410 --> 00:20:20.860
These are not
supported by Spanner.

00:20:20.860 --> 00:20:23.260
And the usual approach
is to rewrite them

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:24.760
as application code.

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:32.510
It goes without
saying that testing

00:20:32.510 --> 00:20:35.720
is fundamental to a
successful migration.

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:39.050
And this can either use
test traffic or parts

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:40.550
of your production load.

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:43.820
If it is test traffic,
it's really very important

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:47.120
to ensure that the test
traffic realistically

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:49.040
models the production setup.

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:51.290
And if it's part of
your production load,

00:20:51.290 --> 00:20:53.880
then it's really important
that the part that you use

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.820
be representative
of your full load.

00:20:56.820 --> 00:20:59.990
And as I mentioned earlier,
it's common for steps 3 and 4

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:00.800
to be intermixed.

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:04.010
So you might have an initial
design and an implementation.

00:21:04.010 --> 00:21:04.760
Try it out.

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:05.660
Find issues.

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:08.030
And then go back
and do some redesign

00:21:08.030 --> 00:21:10.110
and change the implementation.

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:11.600
Maybe you find
performance issues.

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:13.350
You need to tweak the
schema, for example.

00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:14.975
So this is really an
iterative process.

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:20.910
Now we come to
the exciting part,

00:21:20.910 --> 00:21:22.650
actually doing the migration.

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:24.660
So first, a quick comment.

00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:28.000
Not every migration will
have a migration day.

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:31.710
So for example, if you use
incremental data migration,

00:21:31.710 --> 00:21:33.720
then your migration
is essentially

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:36.120
an incremental,
continuous process.

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:38.040
It might have a start
day and an end day,

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:41.340
but that could be weeks,
maybe even months apart.

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:44.700
However, many migrations
do have a target day

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:47.040
for moving or
completing data move

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:48.650
and switching over
to using Spanner.

00:21:48.650 --> 00:21:50.400
And this becomes a
really critical day.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:53.220
Here's where you find out if
all of your planning and testing

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:54.180
have been effective.

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:56.580
And obviously, you
really want migration day

00:21:56.580 --> 00:21:58.780
to be problem-free.

00:21:58.780 --> 00:22:01.800
One of the very important
aspects of migration day

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:04.470
is knowing what to do if
you run into problems.

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:07.620
First, how do we
know if things are OK

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:08.940
or if there is a problem?

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:11.880
What signals and dashboards
should we be looking at?

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:13.770
What thresholds are acceptable?

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:16.020
Who is the decision-maker
for reverting?

00:22:16.020 --> 00:22:17.220
How do we revert?

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:19.140
And it's critical that
the revert process

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:20.430
has been well-tested.

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:23.010
So one thing to
consider, especially

00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:26.370
for gaming applications,
is database warmup.

00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:29.580
So for example, suppose we're
migrating a game platform

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:32.190
from MySQL to Spanner,
and we're going

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:34.020
to launch with a new game.

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:37.470
So we've gone through all of
the tasks of schema migration

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:39.780
and application migration,
but we don't actually

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:42.300
have any data, because
this is a new game.

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:45.360
So our tables are empty.

00:22:45.360 --> 00:22:48.030
Spanner's load-balancing
mechanisms

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:50.370
respond to load signals.

00:22:50.370 --> 00:22:52.870
And we'll talk about this in
a little more detail later.

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:55.380
But the point is that
these mechanisms take time

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:57.480
to respond to load changes.

00:22:57.480 --> 00:22:59.820
And generally, this
is not an issue,

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:01.710
because they respond
fairly quickly,

00:23:01.710 --> 00:23:03.490
on the order of a few minutes.

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:06.270
But if the tables
are empty, then it

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:09.660
can take several iterations of
these load-balancing mechanisms

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:13.500
before we can adapt to the
full load of a game launch.

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:17.280
So to address this, we
need to warmup the tables.

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:19.830
And basically, this
is typically done

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:22.740
by prepopulating the
tables with synthetic data

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:26.670
and adding load to
stimulate Spanner's

00:23:26.670 --> 00:23:29.640
load-balancing mechanisms,
and then the synthetic data

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:31.470
can be deleted.

00:23:31.470 --> 00:23:33.600
If a database is
already populated

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:35.610
with data as part
of a data migration,

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:37.590
then warmup is
usually not needed,

00:23:37.590 --> 00:23:41.520
because there's already enough
splits and enough warmup

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:45.330
of the database because
of the load of the data.

00:23:49.620 --> 00:23:51.600
The next couple
of slides, I'm got

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:54.690
to address some low level
details of migrations,

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:57.180
specifically from
MySQL to Spanner.

00:23:57.180 --> 00:23:59.910
And I want to first
have a look at types.

00:23:59.910 --> 00:24:03.810
So Spanner provides coverage
of the core types of MySQL.

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:05.940
However, you'll
notice that, where

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:09.150
MySQL has many different
integer types, for example,

00:24:09.150 --> 00:24:11.640
Spanner has just one, int64.

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:14.700
So similarly for
floating point numbers

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:17.230
and for chars and text types.

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:19.020
There's just one
float type in Spanner

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:20.670
and there's just
one string type,

00:24:20.670 --> 00:24:23.580
although it's a string
type, but it has a parameter

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:26.730
with a length limit.

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:29.520
MySQL also has a
variety of other types,

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:32.740
such as geometry types,
point, line, and so on.

00:24:32.740 --> 00:24:35.460
Spanner doesn't provide a
corresponding type for these,

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:38.790
and we usually recommend using
either string or bytes here.

00:24:43.430 --> 00:24:47.240
Let's dig a little bit into
the design of primary keys.

00:24:47.240 --> 00:24:49.160
And in order to do
this, I first need

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:51.320
to talk about splits
and their role,

00:24:51.320 --> 00:24:55.820
and then we'll come back and
talk about best practices

00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:57.270
for primary keys.

00:24:57.270 --> 00:24:59.600
So some of the material
here has already

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:01.730
been covered in previous
Spanner meetups,

00:25:01.730 --> 00:25:05.680
but I'll briefly recap the idea.

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:08.500
So in Spanner, the
primary key space

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:11.170
of each table in a
database is partitioned

00:25:11.170 --> 00:25:13.360
into chunks, called splits.

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:16.780
So in this example, the
primary key is an integer

00:25:16.780 --> 00:25:21.380
and the first split ranges from
minus infinity to minus 100,

00:25:21.380 --> 00:25:25.010
the next split from minus
99 to 3,000, and so on.

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:27.880
So the same basic thing
happens across other types,

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:29.770
whether they be
strings or floats,

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:32.200
and also for multi-column
primary keys.

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:38.580
Each zone has a
copy of each split.

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:40.920
So I have a very
simple layout here

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:43.230
for a single-region
Spanner instance,

00:25:43.230 --> 00:25:47.070
where we have three zones,
Zone A, Zone B, and Zone C.

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:49.710
And each zone has a
copy of every split.

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:53.790
So here's Split 0 in Zone
A. Here's Split 0 in Zone B.

00:25:53.790 --> 00:25:55.770
And here's Split 0
in Zone C. And that

00:25:55.770 --> 00:25:59.550
could be distributed somewhat
randomly across the Spanner

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:02.360
servers in each zone.

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:05.440
So the key point is that
each zone has full coverage

00:26:05.440 --> 00:26:07.330
of the key space of a table.

00:26:07.330 --> 00:26:10.240
And indeed, it has a full
copy of the table's data.

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:12.370
So you can think of
each zone as essentially

00:26:12.370 --> 00:26:14.620
a standalone
replica, and Spanner

00:26:14.620 --> 00:26:16.900
ensures that each
replica stays in sync

00:26:16.900 --> 00:26:20.430
and has exactly the
same committed data.

00:26:20.430 --> 00:26:23.310
So what's the role of splits?

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:25.600
Well, there were really
two parts to this.

00:26:25.600 --> 00:26:30.360
The first is that splits are
the basis for distributing table

00:26:30.360 --> 00:26:33.030
data across Spanner servers.

00:26:33.030 --> 00:26:37.200
And the second is that they
form a core part of an automated

00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:40.980
sharding mechanism to balance
load across Spanner servers.

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:45.480
So splits are not static,
unlike in many other databases

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:48.280
and sharding databases.

00:26:48.280 --> 00:26:50.070
So in Spanner, splits
are not static.

00:26:50.070 --> 00:26:52.950
They're continually on the
move and they're continually

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:54.040
evolving.

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:58.470
So for example, if a split is
hot and receiving lots of load

00:26:58.470 --> 00:27:01.890
or contains lots of data, it'll
be split into smaller splits.

00:27:01.890 --> 00:27:04.860
And if two neighboring splits
have been relatively inactive

00:27:04.860 --> 00:27:07.090
for a long time, then
they'll be merged.

00:27:07.090 --> 00:27:10.020
So the core ideas behind
splits were actually

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:12.030
developed as part of
Bigtable many years ago,

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:13.920
and they've been
adopted and extended

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:17.310
to form a core part of Spanner.

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:19.260
Based on our
understanding of splits,

00:27:19.260 --> 00:27:21.700
we can come back
to the question,

00:27:21.700 --> 00:27:23.370
what makes a good primary key?

00:27:23.370 --> 00:27:25.530
And very simply put,
a good primary key

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:29.610
is one that spreads the key
values over the key space.

00:27:29.610 --> 00:27:32.460
So names are usually
a good primary key,

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:35.790
unless everyone has the same
name or very similar names.

00:27:35.790 --> 00:27:38.700
And hashes are also an
excellent primary key.

00:27:38.700 --> 00:27:44.120
What we want to avoid is
primary keys that clump together

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:49.610
some aspect of the
lifecycle of table rows,

00:27:49.610 --> 00:27:53.570
whether that be creation of
rows, or updates and reads,

00:27:53.570 --> 00:27:54.750
or deletions.

00:27:54.750 --> 00:27:57.260
So for example,
auto-increment keys

00:27:57.260 --> 00:28:00.890
are problematic, because with
auto-increment, all new rows

00:28:00.890 --> 00:28:04.100
will be in exactly the
same part of the key space.

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:07.560
So this will cause a hot
spot for row creation

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:09.770
for inserting into the database.

00:28:09.770 --> 00:28:12.260
And in fact, with
auto-increment, they'll

00:28:12.260 --> 00:28:14.210
actually appear
in the same split

00:28:14.210 --> 00:28:16.655
and it'll be held in
the same Spanner server.

00:28:16.655 --> 00:28:20.030
So more generally, we want
to design our primary keys

00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:21.155
to avoid hot spots.

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:27.290
I mentioned several tools
for assisting with migration,

00:28:27.290 --> 00:28:29.630
and I'm going to conclude
with a little more detail

00:28:29.630 --> 00:28:33.110
of these tools and
their capabilities.

00:28:33.110 --> 00:28:35.450
I've mentioned
HarbourBridge several times.

00:28:35.450 --> 00:28:37.590
So HarbourBridge is
an open-source tool.

00:28:37.590 --> 00:28:40.850
It's used for various
stages of migration.

00:28:40.850 --> 00:28:42.710
It can be used for assessment.

00:28:42.710 --> 00:28:46.350
It can be used for schema
migration, for data migration.

00:28:46.350 --> 00:28:50.090
So here's a sample workflow,
where HarbourBridge is run

00:28:50.090 --> 00:28:53.840
and it ingests data from a
MySQL or Postgres database.

00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:55.520
And we can either
use dump formats,

00:28:55.520 --> 00:28:58.490
like mysqldump or
pg_dump, or directly

00:28:58.490 --> 00:29:00.890
access the database
using SELECT queries

00:29:00.890 --> 00:29:03.860
and extract schema information
using information schema

00:29:03.860 --> 00:29:04.940
tables.

00:29:04.940 --> 00:29:07.940
It builds a Spanner
schema, creates a database,

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:11.720
and populates it with data
from the source database.

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:15.410
And then it documents that
run with a detailed assessment

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:19.250
report that includes potential
issues with type mappings,

00:29:19.250 --> 00:29:22.940
highlights MySQL features
that are used by the source

00:29:22.940 --> 00:29:25.140
database that are not
supported by Spanner,

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:27.950
and also logs any
errors encountered.

00:29:27.950 --> 00:29:32.960
So HarbourBridge can also be
used to browse and customize

00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:36.950
your Spanner schema in what
we call Schema Assistant mode.

00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:44.610
The other two tools I want to
highlight focus on data import.

00:29:44.610 --> 00:29:49.080
The first is a bulk
data import tool,

00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:53.280
and this is part of Google's
generic Dataflow service, which

00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:56.490
is a unified, streaming, and
batch processing service.

00:29:56.490 --> 00:30:01.140
So this service supports bulk
data import into Spanner.

00:30:01.140 --> 00:30:05.760
And supported formats
include Avro and CSV.

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:09.400
And the second tool I want to
mention is a CDC import tool.

00:30:09.400 --> 00:30:12.030
So this is part of
our generic DataStream

00:30:12.030 --> 00:30:15.030
service, which is a change
data capture and replication

00:30:15.030 --> 00:30:16.120
service.

00:30:16.120 --> 00:30:20.190
And this service supports
MySQL to Spanner CDC,

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:22.680
amongst other combinations.

00:30:22.680 --> 00:30:25.650
Let me stop here, and many
thanks for your attention.

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:28.500
And I'll invite
questions and comments.

00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:34.950
And let me go over to the chat.

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:39.720
And I think there was at least
one question there from Andrew.

00:30:46.570 --> 00:30:48.460
So Andrew asks,
would you advocate

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:50.620
any small-scale
migration testing

00:30:50.620 --> 00:30:52.870
using the Spanner emulator?

00:30:52.870 --> 00:30:54.070
It's actually two questions.

00:30:54.070 --> 00:30:55.480
Let me talk about the emulator.

00:30:55.480 --> 00:31:01.510
So the emulator is a
really fantastic piece

00:31:01.510 --> 00:31:05.170
of technology for testing
things out and testing out

00:31:05.170 --> 00:31:06.490
functionality.

00:31:06.490 --> 00:31:13.930
I would be careful,
though, of what--

00:31:13.930 --> 00:31:18.310
if you're doing a migration,
then you typically

00:31:18.310 --> 00:31:24.340
want to understand throughput
and performance issues.

00:31:24.340 --> 00:31:26.930
And the emulator is not
going to help you with those.

00:31:26.930 --> 00:31:31.780
So view the emulator as a
really faithful reproduction

00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:34.840
of Spanner's functionality--
so for testing code,

00:31:34.840 --> 00:31:36.820
testing different scenarios.

00:31:36.820 --> 00:31:41.050
But usually in a
migration, as I said,

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:44.230
you're really interested in
understanding scalability,

00:31:44.230 --> 00:31:46.390
throughput, and performance.

00:31:46.390 --> 00:31:48.980
And the emulator is not going
to be able to help you there.

00:31:48.980 --> 00:31:51.010
So a qualified yes.

00:31:51.010 --> 00:31:53.690
Definitely use the emulator
where it's appropriate.

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:57.070
But I would not encourage
using the emulator

00:31:57.070 --> 00:32:01.750
as your only testing environment
for migration testing.

00:32:01.750 --> 00:32:04.660
OK, how would you handle
application code changes

00:32:04.660 --> 00:32:08.480
for transactions that hit
the 20,000 mutation limit?

00:32:08.480 --> 00:32:11.180
Yeah, that's an
interesting question.

00:32:11.180 --> 00:32:14.950
So I guess I have a
question in response,

00:32:14.950 --> 00:32:22.230
and that is there are
a number of other tools

00:32:22.230 --> 00:32:26.280
that we have for doing
large-scale mutations.

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:33.600
And in particular, we have
ways of doing partitioned DML.

00:32:33.600 --> 00:32:36.540
The partitioned DML
is typically used

00:32:36.540 --> 00:32:40.560
when you want to make
a large-scale change

00:32:40.560 --> 00:32:43.230
across the entire database.

00:32:43.230 --> 00:32:47.490
And the partitioned DML allows
you to basically carve up

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:53.310
the overall operation that you
want to do into smaller pieces.

00:32:53.310 --> 00:33:00.300
And so that way, we get around
the 20,000 mutation limit,

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:03.000
because we're actually
splitting up the operation

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:06.150
into multiple transactions.

00:33:06.150 --> 00:33:08.730
So if you can use
it, partitioned

00:33:08.730 --> 00:33:11.670
DML is definitely a
very convenient tool

00:33:11.670 --> 00:33:14.250
and will solve the
problem you have.

00:33:14.250 --> 00:33:17.640
The main constraints
around partitioned

00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:26.220
DML are the restrictions on
what kinds of DML operations

00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:28.120
you can use it for.

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:32.280
So if you've got something that
you can't use partitioned DML,

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:37.920
then the main operation
or the main approach

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:40.860
that's open to you is
to basically split it up

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:43.630
into smaller sets of mutations.

00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:48.180
So basically, split it up
into smaller transactions.

00:33:48.180 --> 00:33:52.815
And that may require some code
change or some manual teasing

00:33:52.815 --> 00:33:56.100
apart of this large operation.

00:33:56.100 --> 00:33:58.960
So maybe Andrew, do you
have some more background

00:33:58.960 --> 00:34:01.440
you can give on the particular
use case that you have?

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:09.380
OK.

00:34:12.690 --> 00:34:14.880
And Andrew also mentions--

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:16.739
OK, no problem.

00:34:16.739 --> 00:34:19.260
Andrew also mentions in
the data streaming space,

00:34:19.260 --> 00:34:20.475
there's [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:23.159 --> 00:34:28.530
Any other questions
live or anything anyone

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:33.449
wants to either ask about
or to give feedback on?

00:34:38.260 --> 00:34:39.909
Yes.

00:34:39.909 --> 00:34:43.510
[? Swara ?] asks, will
there be any SQL rewrite

00:34:43.510 --> 00:34:45.909
required from MySQL to Spanner?

00:34:45.909 --> 00:34:52.510
Yeah, I mentioned that part of a
migration from MySQL to Spanner

00:34:52.510 --> 00:34:57.760
can potentially involve
having to rewrite SQL queries.

00:34:57.760 --> 00:35:03.320
Spanner and MySQL have somewhat
different SQL dialects.

00:35:03.320 --> 00:35:08.500
And so if you're using
very basic SQL constructs,

00:35:08.500 --> 00:35:14.590
those may well be
fairly easy or maybe not

00:35:14.590 --> 00:35:15.850
require change at all.

00:35:15.850 --> 00:35:19.540
But it's quite common
for MySQL applications

00:35:19.540 --> 00:35:25.780
that use a lot of
different SQL constructs.

00:35:25.780 --> 00:35:31.030
That requires a substantial
amount of rewrite.

00:35:31.030 --> 00:35:34.780
OK, so the follow-up question.

00:35:34.780 --> 00:35:36.910
How much percentage
do we need to cover up

00:35:36.910 --> 00:35:38.935
on the migration strategy?

00:35:38.935 --> 00:35:40.282
I"m not quite sure I answered--

00:35:40.282 --> 00:35:41.990
I'm not quite sure I
follow the question.

00:35:41.990 --> 00:35:47.260
Do you mean how much is the
query rewrite of the migration?

00:35:47.260 --> 00:35:48.400
Or are you referring--

00:35:51.970 --> 00:35:53.800
[? Swara, ?] do you
mind clarifying that?

00:35:53.800 --> 00:35:54.970
Oh, it's a SQL rewrite.

00:35:54.970 --> 00:36:03.220
Yes, that depends tremendously
on the particular application.

00:36:03.220 --> 00:36:07.990
So I have seen application
migrations where the SQL

00:36:07.990 --> 00:36:11.020
rewrite was very
straightforward,

00:36:11.020 --> 00:36:13.180
and particular
applications that were

00:36:13.180 --> 00:36:17.470
organized around some sort of
database abstraction layer.

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:20.710
I've heard a number
of Spanner users

00:36:20.710 --> 00:36:23.710
who were very
easily able to move

00:36:23.710 --> 00:36:26.680
from MySQL over to Spanner,
from a query perspective.

00:36:26.680 --> 00:36:30.640
But I also know customers who
have spent a substantial amount

00:36:30.640 --> 00:36:32.320
of time on this.

00:36:32.320 --> 00:36:35.680
So it's hard to give
an exact percentage,

00:36:35.680 --> 00:36:39.440
because there's a huge variation
across different customers.

00:36:39.440 --> 00:36:44.590
So I think some
good starting points

00:36:44.590 --> 00:36:47.300
in estimating this
are around, well,

00:36:47.300 --> 00:36:49.840
how many different
queries do you have?

00:36:49.840 --> 00:36:52.210
And how many different
kinds of queries?

00:36:52.210 --> 00:36:56.950
And what kinds of features
of Spanner are those--

00:36:56.950 --> 00:37:01.180
sorry, what kinds of features of
MySQL are those queries using?

00:37:01.180 --> 00:37:04.360
And then look to see at how
the features that you're

00:37:04.360 --> 00:37:07.930
using in your MySQL queries--

00:37:07.930 --> 00:37:11.060
how those features map
onto Spanner features.

00:37:11.060 --> 00:37:15.460
So if you're doing very simple
things like standard SELECTs,

00:37:15.460 --> 00:37:19.930
then that's going to be very
easy to migrate over to Spanner

00:37:19.930 --> 00:37:22.060
and may not require
any changes at all.

00:37:22.060 --> 00:37:29.320
If you're using a lot of
internal MySQL operations, so

00:37:29.320 --> 00:37:31.000
built-in functions--
if you're using

00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:33.430
a lot of built-in
functions, then you'll

00:37:33.430 --> 00:37:35.980
need to see how they
map over to Spanner.

00:37:35.980 --> 00:37:40.030
Sometimes, the syntax
is completely identical.

00:37:40.030 --> 00:37:42.010
Sometimes, for
built-in functions,

00:37:42.010 --> 00:37:44.227
there are variations
in the naming

00:37:44.227 --> 00:37:45.310
of the built-in functions.

00:37:45.310 --> 00:37:47.320
And some built-in
functions of MySQL

00:37:47.320 --> 00:37:48.770
are not supported in Spanner.

00:37:48.770 --> 00:37:51.730
So that's another aspect to
look at, to look at the queries

00:37:51.730 --> 00:37:54.370
that you have and
look at the features

00:37:54.370 --> 00:38:00.110
that you're using in MySQL.

00:38:00.110 --> 00:38:03.470
Andrew follows up to
the earlier question.

00:38:03.470 --> 00:38:06.140
To the earlier question,
I think you're mostly

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:09.200
around third-party
applications or ORMs, where

00:38:09.200 --> 00:38:14.120
one can't as easily control
the size of the transaction.

00:38:14.120 --> 00:38:18.350
Yeah, look, I agree with you.

00:38:18.350 --> 00:38:20.660
This is an issue.

00:38:20.660 --> 00:38:23.060
We're more than happy
to work with you

00:38:23.060 --> 00:38:27.740
on finding solutions and
understanding more about it.

00:38:27.740 --> 00:38:33.560
We recognize that this is an
issue and something that we're

00:38:33.560 --> 00:38:38.240
working on to
increase the limits.

00:38:38.240 --> 00:38:39.540
We don't have any commits.

00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:40.563
We don't have any dates.

00:38:40.563 --> 00:38:42.230
We don't have any
announcements on this.

00:38:42.230 --> 00:38:44.190
But we're well
aware of the issue.

00:38:44.190 --> 00:38:46.430
So Andrew, I definitely
strongly encourage you

00:38:46.430 --> 00:38:48.710
to interact with
the Spanner team,

00:38:48.710 --> 00:38:53.030
either with me or other
people in the Spanner team,

00:38:53.030 --> 00:38:55.370
or with your point of
contact on the sales side.

00:38:55.370 --> 00:39:00.110
We'd love to hear more about
the problems that you're having.

00:39:00.110 --> 00:39:02.210
And I completely
agree, when you're

00:39:02.210 --> 00:39:05.210
using a third-party
tool or an ORM,

00:39:05.210 --> 00:39:08.510
you often don't have
the control over this.

00:39:08.510 --> 00:39:10.680
So yeah, please, please
get in touch with us.

00:39:10.680 --> 00:39:13.610
And I think it'll depend on
the specifics of the problem

00:39:13.610 --> 00:39:14.270
that you have.

00:39:19.400 --> 00:39:20.720
Any other questions?

00:39:25.130 --> 00:39:27.280
Thank you, [? Swara ?]
